// A model that MUST NOT COMPILE. It lives outside tests/emit so the normal
// "generated C++ compiles clean" loop does not sweep it up.
//
// §10.5 made `sim` readable from every body. The whole design rests on that
// widening the surface WITHOUT widening it for `sim.stop`/`sim.abort`, which
// §10.2 confines to the mutating methods: a leaf holds a plain `se_rt::RunView`
// member, and `preamble()` shadows it with an `se_rt::RunControl` in `init()`
// and `on_step()` only.
//
// Nothing else in the suite protects that. A refactor that injected
// `RunControl` everywhere -- or that made `stop` a member of `RunView` --
// would leave every other test green while quietly legalising a halt inside a
// method that runs four times per step at trial states the trajectory never
// visits. So this case asserts the negative directly, by compiling it and
// requiring the failure.
//
// The `out.y` line is the positive half of the same assertion: `sim.time()`
// in a pure method MUST compile. The runner therefore requires the failure to
// name `stop` and `RunView` and to say nothing about `time` -- a compile that
// fails for some other reason is not this test passing.
package purestop;

node PureStop {
    outputs { y: double(-); }

    output() {
        sim.stop("halting from a pure method is not legal");   // must NOT compile
        out.y = sim.time();                                    // must compile
    }
}
